  recomputes for all tasks on load and on a ~30s tick. Dismissal is the one persisted piece: an ack map of [38;5;153m{taskId, [39m
  [38;5;153mthreshold}[39m so a dismissed "due soon" doesn't come back, while the later "overdue" still fires.
  [3mBuys:[0m no stale records, no reconciliation. Edit a due date and the alerts are simply correct on the next tick — no
  invalidation logic, because there's nothing to invalidate. Time passing with the tab closed is free: on reload, [38;5;153mnow[39m is
  just bigger. Smallest testable surface — [38;5;153mdueState[39m is a pure function, which means the notification logic gets real
  unit tests without touching the DOM or the clock.
  [3mCosts:[0m no history ("what did I get alerted about last week?") and no natural home for future non-time triggers.
  [3mRules out later:[0m nothing structurally — a log can be added by having the tick write transitions to a store.

  [1mB. Materialized notification log — a real notification store[0m
  Task changes and clock ticks write notification records ([38;5;153m{id, taskId, kind, createdAt, readAt}[39m). The UI reads the
  store. This is the literal "notifications system" from your original request.
  [3mBuys:[0m history, read/unread, and a single place any future trigger type plugs into.
  [3mCosts:[0m every bug class A avoids. Duplicate suppression when the tick re-runs. Reconciliation when a task is deleted or
  its due date moves backward — you now have records referencing a state that no longer exists. Roughly double the code
  for a single-user list.
  [3mWhen it wins:[0m if you expect several trigger types soon, or genuinely want a history view.

  [1mC. Scheduled timers — one [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m per task, armed at its due moment[0m
  No polling; each task schedules its own firing.
  [3mBuys:[0m no idle work, fires at the exact second.
  [3mCosts:[0m timers don't survive a reload, so you need the reconciliation pass from A anyway [3mplus[0m the timer bookkeeping —
  re-arm on every edit, cancel on delete. Nothing fires while the tab is closed, and [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m caps around 24.8 days,
  so far-future tasks need re-arming logic. It's strictly more machinery than A for a benefit (second-level precision)
  that a due-date feature doesn't need.
  [3mI'd discard this one[0m — I include it because "use timers" is the intuitive first answer, and it's worth saying
  explicitly why polling beats it here.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m The pure-function core is the real argument — it makes the notification logic independently
  testable and independently reviewable, which was the isolation promise I made when we collapsed to a single spec. B's
  extra machinery buys history, and I don't think you've asked for history. If you want it later, A grows into B without
  a rewrite.

